#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""MapperX — field map and route planner in the browser (run on the PC).

    python3 field_planner.py

  1. Enter the real field size in cm, draw walls, obstacles, podiums, zones
     and objects (drag a rectangle, then type exact cm values in the panel).
     A top-down photo or plan of the field can be put underneath.
  2. Set the robot start point and the direction it faces at Start.
  3. Click the route points. Each point: drive forward or back, barrier()
     (obstacle avoidance), stop by the front Sharp instead of the encoder,
     and actions after arriving (visionPickup, putDownObject, turn, drive by
     the side ultrasonics, ...).
  4. The planner simulates the robot with the same sensor logic as
     DriveMotor (side ultrasonics and the front Sharp measure distances to
     the drawn walls), shows collisions and gives ready "case" code: paste it
     into the switch (stateAutomatic) in DriveMotor.java and deploy.

Maps are saved in field_maps/ next to this script (JSON, with the photo).

Studios (menu that drops from the top edge, or Alt+1..7):
  MapperX     the field map and route (above)
  DetectX     camera frames and HSV color profiles (was calibrate_hsv_pc.py)
  TuneX       every constant / Tunable of the project, edited in place
  ConstructX  block code builder -> Java functions in DriveMotor.java
  BoardX      Shuffleboard: live NetworkTables values (pip install pynetworktables)
  PinX        wiring (servo/DIO/analog/CAN channels) read from the Java code
  PitX        before a run: checklist, calibration wizard, route slots
  VaultX      the whole project packed inside this file + build / deploy
  MapperX map also shows the robot live (NetworkTables "MapperX/telemetry")
  and replays recorded runs (robot: /home/lvuser/runs, served on port 1186).

VS Code: run with --no-browser (or the "MapperX" task), then
Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+P → "Simple Browser: Show" → http://127.0.0.1:8765/
"""

